{"name":"Lilac Time","hex":"#a4abbf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lilac","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large shrub of the genus Syringa, especially Syringa vulgaris, bearing white, pale-pink, or purple flowers.","A flower of the lilac shrub."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour.","Of a cat or its fur: having a pale brown colour, lighter than chocolate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lilac"},{"term":"time","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The inevitable progression into the future with the passing of present and past events.","The feeling of the passage of events and their relative duration, as experienced by an individual."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To measure or record the time, duration, or rate of something.","To choose when something commences or its duration."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/time"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jane Cowl","blocks":[{"text":"Jane Cowl was an American film and stage actress and playwright who was, in the words of author Anthony Slide, \"notorious for playing lachrymose parts\". Actress Jane Russell was named in Cowl's honor."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Jane_Cowl%2C_stage_actress_%28SAYRE_23254%29.jpg/330px-Jane_Cowl%2C_stage_actress_%28SAYRE_23254%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jane_Cowl","wikidata":"Q3616176"},{"title":"Das Dreimäderlhaus","blocks":[{"text":"Das Dreimäderlhaus, adapted into English-language versions as Blossom Time and Lilac Time, is a Viennese pastiche operetta with music by Franz Schubert, rearranged by Heinrich Berté (1857–1924), and a libretto by Alfred Maria Willner and Heinz Reichert. The work gives a fictionalized account of Schubert's romantic life, and the story was adapted from the 1912 novel Schwammerl by Rudolf Hans Bartsch (1873–1952). Originally the score was mostly Berté, with just one piece of Schubert's, but the producers required Berté to discard his score and create a pasticcio of Schubert music."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/93/DasD1916.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Das_Dreim%C3%A4derlhaus","wikidata":"Q832620"},{"title":"Lilac Time (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Lilac Time is a 1928 American synchronized sound romantic war film directed by George Fitzmaurice and starring Colleen Moore and Gary Cooper. While the film has no audible dialog, it was released with a synchronized musical score with sound effects using the short-lived Firnatone process. After First National Pictures was taken over by Warner Bros. Pictures, the soundtrack was transferred to sound-on-disc Vitaphone discs and re-released in that format. The film is about young American aviators fighting for Britain during World War I who are billeted in a field next to a farmhouse in France. The daughter who lives on the farm meets one of the new aviators who is attracted to her. As the flyers head off on a mission, the young aviator promises to return to her."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Lilac_Time_theatrical_poster.jpg/330px-Lilac_Time_theatrical_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lilac_Time_(film)","wikidata":"Q3829070"},{"title":"Syringa","blocks":[{"text":"Syringa is a genus of 12 currently recognized species of flowering woody plants in the olive family known colloquially as lilacs. They are native to woodland and scrub from southeastern Europe to eastern Asia, and widely and commonly cultivated in temperate areas elsewhere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Stockholm-lilac.jpg/330px-Stockholm-lilac.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syringa","wikidata":"Q157449"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Olympic_in_New_York_cropped.jpg/500px-Olympic_in_New_York_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Olympic_in_New_York_cropped.jpg/1280px-Olympic_in_New_York_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Olympic_in_New_York_cropped.jpg/500px-Olympic_in_New_York_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"RMS Olympic June 1911 at New York, end of maiden voyage. Jane travelled from Southampton-to-New York for the maiden voyage.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOlympic_in_New_York_cropped.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Jane_Cowl_2.jpg/250px-Jane_Cowl_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Jane_Cowl_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Jane_Cowl_2.jpg/250px-Jane_Cowl_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Photoplay: the Aristocrat of Motion Picture Magazines, Volume 9 1915","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJane_Cowl_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Jane_Cowl_in_%E2%80%9CThe_Garden_of_Lies%E2%80%9D.jpg/500px-Jane_Cowl_in_%E2%80%9CThe_Garden_of_Lies%E2%80%9D.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Jane_Cowl_in_%E2%80%9CThe_Garden_of_Lies%E2%80%9D.jpg/1280px-Jane_Cowl_in_%E2%80%9CThe_Garden_of_Lies%E2%80%9D.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut